lines=['# Complete replacement: Linux through 96 threads and macOS options','','This follow-up measures the **complete** replacement at `8f66b3d921c9512e1e5968f494c77715ff71c696` against the previously reviewed optimized Xerces control `58aa37348c968a12e3285295f28c197cc0b7bdbe`, not the old libxml2 PoC. Later feature-branch commits only publish documents. Both Linux backends were rebuilt with GCC 13.3, C++23 and `-O3 -DNDEBUG`, using private unpatched libxml2 2.15.4 / Xerces 3.3.0 and the same dependencies, inputs and harness. The full-replacement process maps no Xerces library.','','Linux uses ibminode05, EPYC 7763: 64 physical cores / 128 hardware threads, with CPUs 0–95 available to the timed processes. Thus 96 includes 32 SMT siblings. Each measured cell has five randomized fresh-process runs; inputs are cached, not a cold-storage benchmark. Independent-reader tests use OpenMP/BLAS=1; separate fixed-work tests vary the requested OpenMP team through 96. Workers in each cell read the same cached file and deterministic query sequence, each owning its own state; this is neither a 96-file storage test nor proof of shared-object thread safety.','','The fresh Linux matrix has **2,280 process runs**: six-input streaming/indexed/open workloads, whole-document loads, retained fast/default contexts, fixed-work OpenMP and a paired declaration control. There are **280 additional macOS build/encoding/source-option runs**. [One combined table](all-comparisons.csv) contains '+f"**{table['total_cells']:,} summary cells** across historical and fresh phases, with source/build/library/patch identities; **{table['new_process_records']:,} new process records** are included. `seconds_median` is whole-workload time; `job_open_seconds_median` isolates opening. Historical data remain unchanged.",'','## Linux performance','','| Input | Backend | Stream files/s, 1 reader | Stream files/s, 96 readers | Best stream files/s (readers) |','|---|---|---:|---:|---:|']
